syntax: read pending here-documents after the first newline in a subshell

A subshell set aside the here-documents pending when it started, as a
command substitution does, so their bodies were only read after its
closing parenthesis. bash, dash, mksh, zsh and ksh93 read them after the
first newline, wherever it is:

	$ printf 'cat <<EOF && (\nbar\nEOF\n\tfoo\n)\n' | bash -n    # valid
	$ printf 'cat <<EOF && (\nfoo\n)\nbar\nEOF\n' | bash -n
	bash: line 6: syntax error: unexpected end of file from `(' command on line 1

The parser rejected the first, reading bar and EOF as commands and the
here-document as unclosed, and accepted the second. A subshell now
leaves pending here-documents to be read at its first newline; a
command substitution still postpones them, as bash does.

Reproducer, before this change: `cat <<EOF && (\nbar\nEOF\n\tfoo\n)`
failed with "1:5: unclosed here-document `EOF`".

